  {
   "vo": "Why did it work? First, he owned the middle overs: almost all his deliveries came between overs seven and fifteen, when India wanted to accelerate. Second, it didn't matter which hand you batted with. Left-handers struck at one oh nine against him, right-handers at one twenty-six. And third, this wasn't about spin in general. Bracewell's off-spin went for twelve an over.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "list",
    "head": {
     "t": "WHY HE WORKED"
    },
    "items": [
     {
      "h": "HE OWNED THE MIDDLE OVERS",
      "d": "108 of his 114 balls came in overs 7–15",
      "at": 0.06
     },
     {
      "h": "BOTH HANDS, SAME RESULT",
      "d": "Left-handers SR 109 · right-handers SR 126",
      "at": 0.38
     },
     {
      "h": "NOT 'SPIN' IN GENERAL",
      "d": "Bracewell's off-spin went at 12.17 an over",
      "c": "red",
      "at": 0.75
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "vo": "Game by game, he was a constant threat: two for thirty-nine, then four for twenty-one, one for seventeen, and two for twenty-four. Only in the final match did India finally get after him, nought for thirty-nine. But by then, New Zealand had found their rhythm and won the last two games.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "timeline",
    "head": {
